            "bill_text": "(1) Where a registered professional becomes aware of a serious public safety risk arising from built environment work \u2014 whether their own or that of another professional \u2014 they shall report the risk to the Council immediately and, where immediate danger exists, to the relevant county government and emergency services.\n(2) A professional who makes a report in good faith under this section shall not be subject to disciplinary action or civil liability for making the report.\n(3) A professional who fails to report a known serious public safety risk, without reasonable cause, commits professional misconduct.\n(4) For the purposes of this section, a \"serious public safety risk\" means any structural defect, fire safety system failure, hazardous material use, or other condition in a built structure or infrastructure that, on the professional's reasonable assessment, creates a probability of injury to or death of a member of the public if not remedied within a reasonable period. A risk shall be presumed serious if it involves\u2014\n(a) load-bearing structural elements exhibiting cracking, deflection, or deformation beyond design tolerances;\n(b) fire safety systems that are non-functional or have not been commissioned;\n(c) use of materials known to be toxic, substandard, or counterfeit in structural or life-safety applications;\n(d) illegal additional floors or structural modifications not reflected in approved drawings; or\n(e) any condition identified as high-risk by the National Building Code or relevant Kenya Standards.\n(5) The Council shall maintain a publicly accessible registry of all serious public safety risks reported under this section, with identifying information anonymized pending investigation.",
